## Commit

`6e49b530c9aafbff54f18eb05c1d698b973e0b61` — "add slugify helper" (on `existing-feature`)

```
 src/slugify.js      | 12 ++++++++++++
 src/slugify.test.js | 34 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 46 insertions(+)
```

The commit contains only my two files — nothing from the concurrent agent leaked in. Two notes on the shared-index handling: `git status --short` before committing showed only my two staged paths, and I committed with an explicit pathspec (`git commit -m "..." -- src/slugify.js src/slugify.test.js`) rather than a bare `git commit`, so a concurrent stage landing between the status check and the commit still could not have been swept in. No `index.lock` contention occurred, so no retries were needed. Per project policy the message is a plain imperative subject with no attribution or co-author trailer.
